{
  "abstract": "We give a component-level capacity bound for directed strongly regular graphs with equal adjacent and nonadjacent two-walk counts. The standard symmetric/skew identities force the symmetric part to preserve each squared skew-frequency space. Projecting actual binary cross-block rows onto such a space bounds how much energy each other component can supply. We calculate exact bounds for explicit six- and nine-vertex one-way components, including all local reciprocal completions and complete binary-row projection distributions. For DSRG(45,18,16,7,7), each type must occur at least four times if its frequency occurs in no other component type. Hence among supports made entirely from these two types, only five copies of the nine-vertex component remain possible. The mixed supports with component counts (3,3) and (6,1) are excluded even when every reciprocal edge is free. A known order-12 graph attains the six-vertex cross-block bound. As an application, exact diagonal return counts force every binary two-sided equitable triple lift of an explicit trace-twelve weighted quotient into one of the excluded supports. Independent local orbit enumeration and standard-library rational-arithmetic checks verify the finite ingredients. The proof does not use completion-solver outcomes. These are conditional component and quotient restrictions; the unrestricted order-45 existence question remains open.",
